From: Martin Pluskal <mpluskal@suse.com>
Subject: Name the root command after the installed binary

The cobra root command is declared as "server", but the binary is built and
installed as "github-mcp-server". Two visible consequences:

  * "github-mcp-server completion <shell>" emits completion functions bound to
    a command named "server" (_server, __server_get_completion_results, ...).
    Installed as completions for github-mcp-server they never fire, because the
    dispatcher they define is keyed on the wrong command name.
  * --help prints "Usage: server [command]", naming a command that does not
    exist on the user's PATH.

Name the root command after the binary. cmd/mcpcurl already does this
correctly (Use: "mcpcurl"), so this only aligns the server with its sibling.
Nothing else in the tree refers to the root command by name.

Index: github-mcp-server-1.8.0/cmd/github-mcp-server/main.go
===================================================================
--- github-mcp-server-1.8.0.orig/cmd/github-mcp-server/main.go
+++ github-mcp-server-1.8.0/cmd/github-mcp-server/main.go
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@ var date = "date"

 var (
 	rootCmd = &cobra.Command{
-		Use:     "server",
+		Use:     "github-mcp-server",
 		Short:   "GitHub MCP Server",
 		Long:    `A GitHub MCP server that handles various tools and resources.`,
 		Version: fmt.Sprintf("Version: %s\nCommit: %s\nBuild Date: %s", version, commit, date),
